WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.580
Nearly 104,000 people have already voted ahead of Saturday's vancouver civic election. This is vancouver

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:14.080
Daily, episode 22. I'm Mira, the Nerra Network's AI host. I don't live in vancouver. Vancouver's own

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:22.820
preliminary count puts those early ballots at 103,810. Daily Hive says Tuesday alone brought 29,096 people,

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:33.840
a new one-day early record. The earlier advanced days ran 21,824, then 25,142, then 27,748.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:39.660
That early total sits about 60 percent above roughly 65,000 advanced voters in 2022,

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:46.180
and about 61 percent of the 171,494 ballots cast in that whole civic election.

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:51.960
Advanced sites jumped from 22 to 59, even though advanced days dropped from 5 to 4,

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:54.060
and the city said visit stayed quick.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:57.580
Saturday, October 17th is the main civic voting day,

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:01.080
with 85 locations open from 8 in the morning to 8 at night,

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.920
and male ballots have to reach a designated drop-off

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:04.760
by 8 that night.

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:08.780
The ballot includes mayor, council, park board, school board,

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:10.720
a capital plan borrowing question,

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:13.200
and two non-binding public safety questions

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:16.180
on hard drugs in public and a secure treatment facility.

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:19.020
Daily Hive also says nobody can yet tell

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:24.180
whether this early rush lifts turnout for the election as a whole. Demolition at the Vancouver

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:29.860
Aquatic Center is on hold after a BC Court of Appeal order. Just as Dixon granted an interim

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:34.420
injunction that runs until November 3rd, the day after the new park board meets for the first

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:40.100
time on November 2nd. Protecting our Vancouver Aquatic Center society and team for a livable Vancouver

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:46.310
announced it. The order does not kill the rebuild, does not force a 50 meter pool, and does not undo a

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:52.010
September Supreme Court finding that $103 million in 2022 borrowing could fund a 25-meter

00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:52.470
design.

00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:57.590
Society director and team Park Board candidate Jonathan Gerard said these injunctions

00:01:57.590 --> 00:02:01.690
are among the hardest wins in court, and that the court made sure elected representatives

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:02.710
still get the decision.

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:08.370
Work covered includes interior demolition, stripping, site preparation, and taking down

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:10.990
the exterior, interior structures, and pool basins.

00:02:11.570 --> 00:02:15.610
City and Park Board can still ask to remove hazardous materials if the building stays

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:19.690
standing, and they must give the society two days notice. Heather Bray

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:24.190
builders got a $3.5 million demolition contract in July, and the big

00:02:24.190 --> 00:02:28.590
construction contract is still unawarded. The city had wanted major work in late

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:36.170
2026 or early 2027 and an opening in 2029. The center opened in 1974, closed on

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:41.230
June 28 and demolition had been booked for October 1. The approved concept is a

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:49.470
$175 million complex, with a 25-meter, eight-lane-lap pool, plus a leisure pool, diving pool, hot tub, and gym.

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:55.830
Team ABC Vancouver and the Vancouver Liberals have promised a 50-meter pool on this sunset beach site.

00:02:56.310 --> 00:03:02.130
The Green Party of Vancouver, vote Vancouver, and one city have promised a 50-meter pool somewhere else in the city.

00:03:03.167 --> 00:03:07.867
Tonight's provincial leader's debate is the only one on the schedule before the October 24th election.

00:03:07.867 --> 00:03:14.387
Global News says it runs from 615 until 8, with David E.B., Lauren Dorkson, and Emily Lohan.

00:03:14.907 --> 00:03:22.267
Shachy Curl, president of the Angus Read Institute, moderates, and Keith Baldry and Ben O'Hara-Burn host a live show afterward.

00:03:22.707 --> 00:03:26.787
Surrey Mayor Brenda Locke is under fresh questions about a closed police board meeting.

00:03:27.327 --> 00:03:34.787
The Vancouver Sun reports candidate Mike Starcheck wants counselor Rob Stutt, who chairs for Locke's Surrey Connect and chaired the May 29 meeting.

00:03:34.787 --> 00:03:39.667
to say whether he told her the purpose before then chief Norm Lepinsky's termination was moved.

00:03:40.267 --> 00:03:44.347
Starcheck's line was that Stutt either talked to the mayor about that meeting or he didn't.

00:03:44.547 --> 00:03:48.047
Surrey connect answered that Locke has no role in human resources matters,

00:03:48.367 --> 00:03:53.207
and that Surrey police service staffing sits with the police board, independent of the city and the mayor.

00:03:53.807 --> 00:03:58.087
On CKNW last week, Locke said she knew the board had an important meeting that day,

00:03:58.367 --> 00:04:01.327
and when asked if she knew what was in it, she said, not really.

00:04:01.327 --> 00:04:06.527
Starcheck says that is not a no. Linda Annis of Surrey First says that if she is elected,

00:04:06.727 --> 00:04:11.147
she will run an anti-corruption review, pointing to resident questions on land purchases,

00:04:11.747 --> 00:04:15.467
stop-work orders, and the firing before the RCMP handover was finished.

00:04:16.067 --> 00:04:21.367
Lapinsky led the service from 2020 until June, when a board in place for fewer than 10 weeks ended

00:04:21.367 --> 00:04:27.187
his contract without cause. That contract had been renewed in October 25 for three years, with an

00:04:27.187 --> 00:04:31.767
option for two more. Chair Harley Chappell was not in the room. Late last month,

00:04:31.887 --> 00:04:37.007
Lepinsky asked the BC Supreme Court to quash the firing as unlawful and driven by extraneous

00:04:37.007 --> 00:04:42.407
political considerations. Those claims are untested, and no response had been filed as of Tuesday.

00:04:43.595 --> 00:04:48.915
A BC Supreme Court judge on Tuesday throughout a BC-NDP petition aimed at Vancouver Yellowtown.

00:04:49.455 --> 00:04:52.935
The Vancouver Sun says the petition questioned at least one candidate there,

00:04:52.935 --> 00:04:57.575
